---
/*
  404 — the request that matched no route at all (plan 34 · §3).

  ⚠ THIS IS THE THIRD 404 ON THE SITE AND THE OTHER TWO ARE NOT BUGS. Both
  dynamic routes already answer for themselves, each for a reason written where
  it applies: `blog/[slug].astro` sets a 404 status and renders "This piece
  isn’t here" inside the site's own chrome, and `admin/people/[slug].astro` does
  the same and says in a comment why it chose that over a redirect. What had no
  owner was the request that reaches no route — a typo, a stale inbound link, a
  deleted path — which until now fell to Astro's bare framework default:
  unstyled, unnavigable, and in a different visual universe from every other
  page here.

  *The alternative that lost:* `Astro.rewrite('/404')` from the dynamic routes,
  to centralise all three. It would flatten two deliberate and
  differently-argued behaviours into one, and both of those comments exist
  because the difference was thought about. Three handlers, three reasons.

  ⚠ AND DO NOT "FIX" `[slug].astro` WHILE YOU ARE HERE. An unknown top-level
  slug drifts home rather than hard-404ing, and that is a decision with a
  comment on it. Plan 31's post-build smoke test already had to establish this
  once: the three deleted lab routes still 302 to `/` and that is correct rather
  than stale cache, proven by the control — `/blog/<nonsense>` still 404s. A
  session arriving here and seeing "no 404 page" will reach for that file. It is
  the wrong file.

  ⚠ NO DATABASE READ, DELIBERATELY. This is the one page a crawler and a bad
  link generate at volume, and it is the only page on the site with no reason to
  cost anything. The two 404 handlers above have already paid for their query by
  the time they know they missed; this one never starts one. That is also why
  there is no search box (a reader who typed the wrong URL cannot search for
  what they meant) and no suggestions.

  Two ways out and no more: the Sky, which is where the site actually begins,
  and the blog, which is what most broken links were pointing into.
*/
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';

// Explicit, because this file is reachable two ways. The adapter renders it
// with a 404 for an unmatched route, but a direct visit to `/404` would
// otherwise answer 200 — a page that says "not here" under a status that says
// "here" is exactly the soft-404 a crawler is entitled to believe.
Astro.response.status = 404;

// A 404 must never be cached as if it were the page that was asked for.
Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 'no-store');
---

<SiteLayout title="Not found" description="That page isn’t here.">
  <div class="mx-auto flex min-h-[60vh] max-w-2xl flex-col items-center justify-center px-5 text-center">
    <p class="text-base-content/70 font-serif text-2xl">That page isn’t here.</p>
    <p class="text-base-content/45 mt-2 font-sans text-sm">
      The link may be off by a letter, or it pointed at something that has since moved.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
      <a href="/" class="text-primary inline-flex items-center gap-1 font-sans text-sm hover:underline">
        <Icon name="ph:caret-left" class="size-3.5" /> The sky
      </a>
      <a href="/blog" class="text-primary inline-flex items-center gap-1 font-sans text-sm hover:underline">
        <Icon name="ph:caret-left" class="size-3.5" /> The blog
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
